64378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501174 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501196 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501228 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064452 1050 1080.0 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064724 1071 "![](images/f5/f504ea91bae5d27b3e7b31d697ed06c9e34d838724e8dafac46e69d84de9c3cf.png)\n\nA gas station has a rectangular gasoline storage tank stored underground. The tank is $15\\text{ feet}$ long and $10\\text{ feet}$ wide. A worker lowers a dip stick into the tank and notices that the depth of the gasoline is decreasing by $2\\text{ feet}$ every hour. How fast is the volume of gasoline decreasing?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186064724 1071 "![](images/f5/f504ea91bae5d27b3e7b31d697ed06c9e34d838724e8dafac46e69d84de9c3cf.png)\n\nA gas station has a rectangular gasoline storage tank stored underground. The tank is $15feet$ long and $10feet$ wide. A worker lowers a dip stick into the tank and notices that the depth of the gasoline is decreasing by $2feet$ every hour. How fast is the volume of gasoline decreasing?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186064724 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the gasoline in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=15\\,\\text{ft},\\,W=10\\,\\text{ft},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{hour}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the gasoline in the tank:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(15)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =150\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gasoline level is decreasing at a rate of $300\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/hour}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186066522 1075 #edb/ref 17592186066524 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186066522 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500976 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186066522 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066526 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068432 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{8z^3}$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068432 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{8z^3}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068432 1074 "We first write $8z^3$ as the product $4z^2 \\cdot 2z$ which contains a perfect square $(4z^2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{8z^3} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{(2z)^2\\cdot 2z} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z} \\\\[3pt] &= \\begin{vmatrix}2z & \\cdot \\sqrt{2z} \\\\[3pt] &= & 2 & \\cdot & z & \\cdot \\sqrt{2z} \\\\[3pt] &= 2 & z\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2z}& \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068432 1074 "We first write $8z^3$ as the product $4z^2 \\cdot 2z$ which contains a perfect square $(4z^2){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{8z^3}&=\\[3pt]\n\t\\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{4z^2 \\cdot 2z}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{(2z)^2\\cdot 2z}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{(2z)^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}&=\\[3pt]\n        |2z|\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}&=\\[3pt]\n        |2|\\cdot|z|\\cdot \\sqrt{2z}&=\\[3pt]\n\t2|z|\\sqrt{2z}&\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068469 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{18y^3}$ given that $y \\geq 0$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068469 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{18y^3}$ given that $y \\geq 0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068469 1074 "We first write $18y^3$ as the product $9y^2\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(9y^2)$. Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{18y^3} &= \\ \\sqrt{9y^2 \\cdot 2y} &= \\ \\sqrt{9y^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\ \\sqrt{(3y)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= \\begin{vmatrix}3y & \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= & 3 & \\cdot & y & \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot & y\\end{vmatrix} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid y &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3 \\cdot \\mid y \\mid \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 3 \\cdot y \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\ &= 3y\\sqrt{2y}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068469 1074 "We first write $18y^3$ as the product $9y^2\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(9y^2).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{18y^3} &= \\\n\t\t\\sqrt{9y^2 \\cdot 2y} &= \\\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{9y^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t\\sqrt{(3y)^2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t|3y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\t\t\n\t\t|3| \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince $y \\geq 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3 \\cdot |y| \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3 \\cdot y \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} &= \\\n\t\t3y\\sqrt{2y}. &\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068676 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{50u^7}$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068676 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{50u^7}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068676 1074 "We first write $50u^7$ as the product $25u^6\\cdot 2u$ which contains a perfect square $(25u^6){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{50u^7} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2\\cdot 2u} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u} \\\\[3pt] &= \\begin{vmatrix}5u^3 & \\cdot\\sqrt{2u} \\\\[3pt] &= & 5 & \\cdot & u^3 & \\cdot\\sqrt{2u} \\\\[3pt] &= \\left.5\\end{vmatrix}u\\right|^3 \\sqrt{2u} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186068676 1074 "We first write $50u^7$ as the product $25u^6\\cdot 2u$ which contains a perfect square $(25u^6){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{50u^7}&=\\[3pt]\n\t\\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{25u^6\\cdot 2u}&=\\[3pt]\n\t\\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2\\cdot 2u}&=\\[3pt]\n\t\\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{\\left(5u^3\\right)^2}\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}&=\\[3pt]\n\t|5u^3|\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}&=\\[3pt]\n\t|5|\\cdot |u^3|\\cdot\\sqrt{2u}&=\\[3pt]\n\t5|u|^3 \\sqrt{2u}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501198 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501221 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501151 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501215 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501222 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501139 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501216 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501143 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186068936 1050 540.0 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186069224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186069224 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{32y^{3}}$ given that $y \\ge 0$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186069224 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{32{y}^{3}}$ given that $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186069224 1074 "We first write $32{y}^{3}$ as the product $16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y$ which contains a perfect square $(16{y}^{2}).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{32{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{16{y}^{2}\\cdot 2y} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}\\cdot 2y} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ |4y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ |4|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot y\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} & = \\\\ 4y\\sqrt{2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186069943 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y$, which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{32y} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\[3pt] &= \\mid 4 \\mid \\cdot \\sqrt{2y} \\\\[3pt] &= 4\\sqrt{2y}& \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186069943 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{ab} &=\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to rewrite the square roots of algebraic expressions.\n\nAs an example, let's suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{32y}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite $32y$ as the product $16 \\cdot 2y,$ which contains a perfect square $(16){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{32y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{16 \\cdot 2y}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{4^2 \\cdot 2y}&\n\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2\\cdot 2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n        \\sqrt{4^2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n        |4|\\cdot \\sqrt{2y}&=\\[3pt]\n\n\t4\\sqrt{2y}&\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186078575 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501142 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186078575 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501164 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186078576 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501146 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186087711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501171 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186087712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501182 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186087712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501213 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095887 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095888 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095889 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095890 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095891 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500599 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500748 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500794 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500856 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500957 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095886 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500794 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095917 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095918 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095919 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095920 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095921 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500555 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500635 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186095916 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500714 13194139535348 175921860454