6 \\div 50% \\\\\n&= 36 \\div \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 72\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186147148 1074 "We are given that the part is $36,$ the percentage is $50\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $50\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{50}{100} = \\dfrac{50\\div 50}{100\\div 50} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =36÷50% \\\\  & =36÷\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =36\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =36\\times 2 \\\\  & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147149 1071 "$34\\%$ of a number is $170$. What is the number?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186147149 1071 "$34\\%$ of a number is $170.$ What is the number?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147149 1074 "We're given that the part is $170$, the percentage is $34\\%$, and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $34\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{34}{100} = \\dfrac{34\\div 2}{100\\div 2} = \\dfrac{17}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 170 \\div 34% \\\\\n&= 170 \\div \\frac{17}{50} \\\\\n&= 170 \\cdot \\frac{50}{17} \\\\\n&= \\frac{170}{17} \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 10 \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186147149 1074 "We're given that the part is $170,$ the percentage is $34\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $34\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{34}{100} = \\dfrac{34\\div 2}{100\\div 2} = \\dfrac{17}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =170÷34% \\\\  & =170÷\\frac{17}{50} \\\\  & =170\\times \\frac{50}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{170}{17}\\times 50 \\\\  & =10\\times 50 \\\\  & =500\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147150 1074 "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of girls in the math club, which is $16$. The percentage is $40\\%$, and the whole is the total number of club members.\n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40\\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= 16 \\div 40% \\\\\n&= 16 \\div \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{2} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 40\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the club has $40$ members." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186147150 1074 "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of girls in the math club, which is $16.$ The percentage is $40\\%,$ and the whole is the total number of club members.\n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{100} = \\dfrac{40\\div 20}{100\\div 20} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =16÷40% \\\\  & =16÷\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =16\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2}\\times 5 \\\\  & =8\\times 5 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the club has $40$ members." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147575 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501178 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147575 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501207 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147576 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501162 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147576 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501211 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147577 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501191 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147577 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501210 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186147657 1050 480.0 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186148781 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n![](images/08/082c6ac53fa8de85b739dde0611943e92cb09d74d07378d6a33043d4b231e88d.svg)\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%.$ \n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =\\text{part}÷\\text{percent} \\\\  & =540÷30% \\\\  & =540÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =540\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{540}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =180\\times 10 \\\\  & =1,800\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186148781 1064 "To find a part given a percent and whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percent} \\times \\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part}\n$$\n\nHowever, we sometimes want to find a whole given a part and a percent. To do this, we can use the relationship between multiplication and division to rewrite the above formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{whole} = \\textrm{part} \\div \\textrm{percent}\n$$\n\nLet's use this formula to find the missing number in the following statement:\n$0$\n\nIn this case, the part is $540$, and the percent is $30\\%$.\n\nWe can express $30\\%$ as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{100} = \\dfrac{30\\div 10}{100\\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nPutting these numbers into our formula for the whole, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nwhole &= part \\div percent \\\\\n&= 540 \\div 30% \\\\\n&= 540 \\div \\frac{3}{10} \\\\\n&= 540 \\cdot \\frac{10}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{540}{3} \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 180 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 1, 800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1,800$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186157310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501186 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159543 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159544 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159545 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159547 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500667 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500698 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500879 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186159542 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500990 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186159542 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500990 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186162390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186162390 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\,0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186162390 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$f(x, y) = {1,\\begin{vmatrix}0 < x < 1, x - 1 < y < 1 - x \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function $f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)$ for $0 < x < 1, x - 1 < y < 1 - x$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186162390 1074 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{x-1}^{1-x}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{x-1}^{1-x} \\\\  & =(1-x)-(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-2x, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,\\,x-1<y<1-x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{