now that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|s| = s.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{|r|}{|s|} = \\dfrac{|r|}{s} .\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179463 1071 "Simplify $9\\sqrt{p - 4} - 7\\sqrt{p - 4} .$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179463 1071 "Simplify $9\\sqrt{p - 4} - 7\\sqrt{p - 4}$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179463 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{p - 4}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9\\sqrt{p - 4} - 7\\sqrt{p - 4} &=  \\\\\n(9 - 7)\\sqrt{p - 4} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{p - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179463 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{p - 4}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\sqrt{p-4}-7\\sqrt{p-4} & = \\\\ (9-7)\\sqrt{p-4} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p-4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179464 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{12x}-\\sqrt{27x}$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179464 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{12x}-\\sqrt{27x}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179464 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{12x} - \\sqrt{27x} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4 \\cdot 3x} - \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 3x} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} - \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} &=  \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} - 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3x} - 3\\sqrt{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{3x} - 3\\sqrt{3x} &=  \\\\\n(2 - 3) \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} &=  \\\\\n(-1) \\cdot \\sqrt{3x} &=  \\\\\n&= - \\sqrt{3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179464 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{12x}-\\sqrt{27x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4\\cdot 3x}-\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x}-\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3x}-3\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3x}-3\\sqrt{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3x}-3\\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ (2-3)\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{3x} & = \\\\ -\\sqrt{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179474 1071 "What is the slope of the line $4x-2y=1$?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179474 1071 "What is the slope of the line $4x-2y=1?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179474 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 1 \\\\\n- 2y &= -4x + 1 \\\\\n2y &= 4x - 1 \\\\\n\\frac{2y}{2} &= \\frac{4x - 1}{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4x}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\ny &= 2x - \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=2$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179474 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+1 \\\\ 2y & =4x-1 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{4x-1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=2.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179475 1074 "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x-3y&=8 \\\\[5pt] 4x-3(0)&=8 \\\\[5pt] 4x&=8 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{4x}{4}&=\\dfrac{8}{4} \\\\[5pt] x&=2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $2$, and we conclude that the line intersects the $x$-axis at\n\n$$\n\\left(2,0\\right)\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179475 1074 "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t4x-3y&=8\\[5pt]\n\t4x-3(0)&=8\\[5pt]\n\t4x&=8\\[5pt]\n\t\\dfrac{4x}{4}&=\\dfrac{8}{4}\\[5pt]\n\tx&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $2,$ and we conclude that the line intersects the $x$-axis at $\\left(2,0\\right).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179476 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 4y &= -8 \\\\\n5(0) - 4y &= -8 \\\\\n- 4y &= -8 \\\\\ny &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $2$, and we conclude that the line intersects the $y$-axis at\n\n$$\n\\left(0,2\\right)\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179476 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4y & =-8 \\\\ 5(0)-4y & =-8 \\\\ -4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $2,$ and we conclude that the line intersects the $y$-axis at $\\left(0,2\\right).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179539 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $5(x-6)^7 = 640$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179539 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $5(x-6)^7 = 640.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186179539 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x-6)^7$ term and then taking the $7$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x-6)^7$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(x - 6)^{7} &= 640 \\\\\n(x - 6)^{7} &= 128\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $7$th root of each side and solve for $x$. Since $n=7$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 6)^{7} &= 128 \\\\\n\\sqrt[7]{(x - 6)^{7}} &= \\sqrt[7]{128} \\\\\nx - 6 &= \\sqrt[7]{2^{7}} \\\\\nx - 6 &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=8$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186179539 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x-6)^7$ term and then taking the $7$th root of each si