lution is $x=5.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is odd, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a,$ we get only one solution:* \n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180733 1064 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0$. Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}$.\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36a^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\mid 6a \\mid &=  \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & a\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\mid a \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0$, and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6 \\mid a \\mid = 6a\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186180733 1064 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification. \n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0.$ Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}.$\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{36{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} & = \\\\ |6a| & = \\\\ |6|\\cdot |a| \\\\ 6|a| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|a|=a.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6|a| = 6a .\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180736 1064 "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0$. Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}$.\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{t^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{(t)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\mid t \\mid.& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0$. To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mid t \\mid = -t\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2$. Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{t^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-t &=  \\\\\n- (-2) &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186180736 1064 "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0.$ Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}.$\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sqrt{t^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\\sqrt{(t)^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t|t|.&\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0.$ To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives $|t| = -t.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}.\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2.$ Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & = \\\\ -(-2) & = \\\\ 2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180739 1064 "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}$. This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x} &=  \\\\\n(5 - 3)\\sqrt{x} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}$\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186180739 1064 "When dealing with sums or differences of square roots, we can combine any terms with the same algebraic expression under the square root symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ This is similar to collecting like terms in a linear expression.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{x}-3\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (5-3)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n5\\sqrt{x} - 3\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt y.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180750 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\sqrt[3]{x} - 2\\sqrt[3]{x} &=  \\\\\n(4 - 2)\\sqrt[3]{x} &=  \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt[3]{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}$.\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186180750 1064 "We can simplify cube root expressions in the same way as with square roots.\n\nFor example, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt[3]x - 2\\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\nWe can simplify our expression by combining the two radicals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt[3]{x}-2\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ (4-2)\\sqrt[3]{x} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt[3]{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs with square roots, we *cannot* combine terms when the expressions under the radical are different. For example, the expression\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-1} + \\sqrt[3]{x+1}\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.$\n\nFinally, it is not possible to combine square root and cube root terms, even if the numbers under the radical symbol are the same! For example, the expression\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt x + \\sqrt[3] x\n$$\n\ncannot be simplified because there is no way of combining $\\sqrt x$ and $\\sqrt[3] x.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180783 1064 "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 8 \\\\\n2x + y - 2x &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= 8 - 2x \\\\\ny &= -2x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186180783 1064 "To determine the slope of a line that is given in standard form, we need to convert the equation to slope-intercept form.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the slope of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nTo write this equation in slope-intercept form, we solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =8 \\\\ 2x+y-2x & =8-2x \\\\ y & =8-2x \\\\ y & =-2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-2.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180784 1064 "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+(0) & =8 \\\\ 2x & =8 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $x$-axis at the point $(4,0).$\n\n- To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+y & =8 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line intersects the $y$-axis at the point $(0,8).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186180784 1064 "Suppose we want to find the $x$- and $y$-intercepts of the following line:\n\n$$\n2x + y = 8\n$$\n\nLet's find each of them in turn.\n\n- To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $