 a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the *square of its radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{kr}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=Kr.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188912 1071 "Tom's spherical basketball has a hole in it. Its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t)$, of the ball. Assume that $k > 0$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186188912 1071 "Tom's spherical basketball has a hole in it. Its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of the ball. Assume that $k>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188912 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186187554 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186188975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188975 1071 "The volume of a cube decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t)$, in this process. Assume that $K > 0$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186188975 1071 "The volume of a cube decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186188975 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cube **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cube* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=6{s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-6k{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cube is given by\n\n$$\nV={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ 3{s}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-6k{s}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{6k{s}^{2}}{3{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=2k>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=-K.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186189375 1064 "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. \nAdditionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then\n\n$$\nI(t) = kt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant.\n\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then\n\n$$\nD(t) = 100.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186189375 1064 "When constructing a differential equation to model a particular phenomenon, we sometimes combine several pieces of information to create the correct model.\n\nConsider the following situation:\n\n*The common cold virus spreads through a particular country. The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t$ (in days) since the outbreak started. Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**.*\n\nWe want to find a differential equation that models $P(t),$ the number of infected people. We note the following:\n\n- The statement describes the *rate* at which the *number of infected people* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity $$\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t}.$$\n- The rate at which the number of infected people increases is **proportional** to the **square of the time** $t.$ Therefore, if $I(t)$ represents the rate at which infections increase, then $$I(t) = kt^2,$$ where $k$ is a positive constant.\n- Additionally, $100$ infected people **recover from the virus each day**. Therefore, if $D(t)$ represents the rate at which infections decrease, then $$D(t) = 100.$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that describes this phenomenon is as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & ={{k{t}^{2}-100}_{⏟}}_{I(t)-D(t)}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAlthough we introduced the functions $I(t)$ and $D(t)$ here, we can usually write down the appropriate differential equation straight away. Let's see an example." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186189439 1064 "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context. \n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186189439 1064 "Let's recall some basic facts from geometry in the $xy$-plane:\n\n- The distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the origin $(0,0)$ equals $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.$\n- The (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $x$-axis equals $|y|.$\n- Similarly, the (smallest) distance between the point $(x,y)$ and the $y$-axis equals $|x|.$\n- The slope of the line that passes through $(x,y)$ and the origin equals $\\dfrac{y}{x}.$\n\nWe sometimes need to use these basic facts when constructing differential equations described in a geometric context.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186194207 1078 #edb/ref 17592186194208 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501147 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501155 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501223 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501141 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501194 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501238 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198356 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501242 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501235 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198378 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501152 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501161 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501172 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186501190 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198483 1050 960.0 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198596 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral\n\n$∬_(D)(2x - 1)(x + y)^{2}dxdy$,\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1, 0)$, $(2,-1)$, $(2, 2)$, and $(1, 3)$." 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186198596 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,0),$ $(2,-1),$ $(2,2),$ and $(1,3).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186198596 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x-1,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,0) & =(2(1)-1,1+0) \\\\  & =(1,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-1) & =(2(2)-1,2+(-1)) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,2) & =(2(2)-1,2+2) \\\\  & =(3,4) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,3) & =(2(1)-1,1+3) \\\\  & =(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/1d/1d5943d2cf79e7c77f6db3e939242d7e31068f46506fa618abd24b6ac813d32a.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x-1)(x+y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{2}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}]}_{1}^{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186199449 1064 "To compute the derivative of a vector function, we calculate the derivative of each component individually. So, if \n\nWe can differentiate vector-valued functions with three components in the same way. \n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\big\\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nis our vector-valued function, then the derivative is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\text{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\text{d}t} = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{ \\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function [math]\\mathbf{f}(t)[/math] is differentiable at some point of its domain *if and only if* each of the components [math]x(t),[/math] [math]y(t),[/math] and [math]z(t)[/math] is differentiable at that point.\n\nFor example, suppose that we are given the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left\\langle t^3, \\:  \\ln(t+3), \\:  \\sqrt{t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute the derivative $\\mathbf f'\\!(t),$ we find the derivative of each component function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{d}{dt}⟨{t}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}(t+3),\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{ln}(t+3)),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 false] [17592186199449 1064 "We can differentiate vector-valued functions with three components in the same way. \n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\big\\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nis our vector-valued function, then the derivative is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\text{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\text{d}t} = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{ \\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\: \\dfrac{ \\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, suppose that we are given the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left\\langle t^3, \\:  \\ln(t+3), \\:  \\sqrt{t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo compute the derivative $\\mathbf f'\\!(t),$ we find the derivative of each component function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{d}{dt}⟨{t}^{3},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3),\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3)),\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{t+3},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186241017 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186241017 1071 "Find the values of $k$ such that the vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$