592186500553 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501133 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501133 1077 "field-2" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501133 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500553 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501133 1079 #uuid "fdb9476a-3489-50b6-afd6-e25393d80f1c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500529 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500556 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500622 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500825 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1075 #edb/ref 17592186500925 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1077 "selection" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1078 #edb/ref 17592186500622 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501134 1079 #uuid "fee5e589-c054-5eb6-8af3-cb3f2d4d13a7" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501125 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1069 "q-97574" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1071 "Consider the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ of the vector space $V$ and the basis $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T:V\\to {R}^{2}$ relative to these bases if \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1072 #uuid "00e54e6a-cd0f-51b4-97b3-2489c07dd2c2" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501135 1074 "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501109 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1069 "q-128989" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1071 "Find the point where the line $9x-3y=7$ intersects the $y$-axis." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1072 #uuid "00f5442f-d34a-5776-aee4-863c683c730e" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501136 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3y & =7 \\\\ 9(0)-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =7 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $y$-intercept is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$ Thus, the line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,-\\frac{7}{3}).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501054 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1069 "q-52394" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1071 "$p\\sqrt{x}+q\\sqrt{x}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1072 #uuid "07885ed6-fd3c-5e89-9b4b-974d90f1b3c9" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501137 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{x}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\sqrt{x}+q\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ p\\cdot \\sqrt{x}+q\\cdot \\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (p+q)\\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501094 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1069 "q-140376" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1071 "Rewrite $-7{x}^{2}-14x+7$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1072 #uuid "07e09125-f910-5338-a71f-7baf6d758234" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501138 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-7.$ So, we take out a factor of $-7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-7({x}^{2}+2x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({x}^{2}+2x-1) & =-7({(x+1)}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+(-7)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501071 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1069 "q-141778" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{25{r}^{3}}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1072 #uuid "0902afad-f2ba-5dbf-8fa2-d595df73885c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501139 1074 "We first write $25{r}^{3}$ as the product $25{r}^{2}\\cdot r$ which contains a perfect square $(25{r}^{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{r}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{25{r}^{2}\\cdot r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{r}^{2}\\cdot r} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5r)^{2}\\cdot r} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5r)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(5r)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ |5r|\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |r|\\cdot \\sqrt{r} & = \\\\ 5|r|\\sqrt{r} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501130 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1069 "q-134504" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{p}+4\\sqrt{p}-6\\sqrt[3]{p}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1072 #uuid "09941e60-aba3-5a69-ab9a-c50e221cd8d0" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501140 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{p}$ terms together, but we cannot combine $\\sqrt[3]{p}$ and $\\sqrt{p}$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{p}+4\\sqrt{p}-6\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}-6\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{p}+(1-6)\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt{p}-5\\sqrt[3]{p} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501083 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501107 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501115 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1069 "q-350802" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1071 "Determine ${f}^{″}(t)$ given that $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{t} \\\\ {e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -2{t}^{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{″}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1072 #uuid "0cc15b80-72fe-59a5-9238-583abab2565e" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501141 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{1-2t})\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{6})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\ -2{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -12{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{1-2t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12{t}^{5})\\,\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3/2}} \\\\ 4{e}^{1-2t} \\\\ -60{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501118 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1069 "q-69434" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1071 "What is $(-20)^{-1}?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1072 #uuid "1615e308-0f55-5459-8614-e5b1b28bfe28" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501142 1074 "The expression $(-20)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-20)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{20}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501032 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1069 "q-2934" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{8{a}^{2}}$ given that $a\\ge 0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1072 #uuid "1704928a-5b6a-5e32-992c-5da9fe38c678" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501143 1074 "We first write $8{a}^{2}$ as the product $4{a}^{2}\\cdot 2$ which contains a perfect square $(4{a}^{2}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{a}^{2}\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2a)^{2}\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2a)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ |2a|\\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |a|\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |a|\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a\\ge 0,$ we have $|a|=a,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |a|\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot a\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & = \\\\ 2a\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501014 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1069 "q-79310" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1071 "A cyclist has a small puncture in one of her tires. The volume of air $V,$ in centimeters cubed, in the tire decreases at a rate that's proportional to the volume at that moment. Every day, the cyclist pumps $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ back into the tire.\nWhat is the differential equation describing the rate of change of the volume of air in the tire  at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in days?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1072 #uuid "1800d774-9d7c-5ba7-a1e5-da93acbae2ee" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501144 1074 "The rate of change in the volume of air per day decreases in proportion to $V$ and increases by $12.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501131 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1069 "q-109747" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{5}(\\lambda +3)^{2},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1072 #uuid "18821d40-33b7-5a84-97bc-878b35f96c25" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501145 1074 "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{2}=3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501106 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1069 "q-171258" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1071 "Express ${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}$ as an improper fraction in its simplest form.\n\n${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1072 #uuid "18e9c87c-d5d7-52d0-922d-e65af5fe041b" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501146 1074 "The expression ${(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $\\frac{2}{9}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{9})}^{-1}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501017 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501098 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1069 "q-349464" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨3{t}^{4}-7t+1,\\,8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(t)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1072 #uuid "19691404-0bb9-5165-a3cc-ce9a79557b1b" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501147 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=3{t}^{4}-7t+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=12{t}^{3}-7.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{3}-7,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501033 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1069 "q-128961" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1071 "What is the slope of the line $8x+2y=-7?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1072 #uuid "1bd18cb1-6693-5a86-9b4f-9983e714337e" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501148 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+2y & =-7 \\\\ 2y & =-8x-7 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{-8x-7}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{-8x}{2}-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =-4x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-4.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501101 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1069 "q-343214" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1071 "![](images/7c/7c11298d151c2f93fbe7d00352386ff859f788ced2641ebe724fd7ddd9f7d205.png)\n\nA cube-shaped inflatable toy is being filled so that its surface area is growing at a rate of $150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is its edge increasing when each edge is $5\\text{ in}?$\n\nThe cube's edge is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1072 #uuid "1c009d1d-9f23-53f4-ac53-e3aab6d30ee8" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501149 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 150 & =12(5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are increasing at a rate of $2.5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501037 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1069 "q-130277" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1072 #uuid "1c3c8fd2-c84f-5ffa-82e1-4f53ac4509f6" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501150 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501081 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1069 "q-64060" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1071 "Solve the equation $3\\cdot {2}^{y}=24.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1072 #uuid "21ccfd77-b3db-57e5-9298-b5b4966f212d" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501151 1074 "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by dividing both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {2}^{y} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{y}}{3} & =\\frac{24}{3} \\\\ {2}^{y} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $8$ as ${2}^{3}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{y} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{y} & ={2}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{y} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=3.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501042 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1069 "q-161385" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,{v}^{3},\\,uv⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.$*" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1072 #uuid "2343ccc6-302e-57d1-903e-91d537e6ff5c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501152 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨-2x,\\,-2y,\\,-z⟩{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y={v}^{3}\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-2{u}^{3},\\,-2{v}^{3},\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-3{v}^{3},\\,-3{u}^{3},\\,9{u}^{2}{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(-2{u}^{3})\\cdot (-3{v}^{3})+(-2{v}^{3})\\cdot (-3{u}^{3})+(-uv)\\cdot 9{u}^{2}{v}^{2} \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}+6{u}^{3}{v}^{3}-9{u}^{3}{v}^{3} \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{3}{v}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501091 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1069 "q-110484" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1072 #uuid "294d3a87-ec82-5ba0-946d-47e92c2e2260" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501153 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501100 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1069 "q-34203" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1071 "Simplify $8\\sqrt{a}+9\\sqrt[3]{a}+3\\sqrt[3]{a}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1072 #uuid "2b536617-f99c-5ac2-ab20-131751027d16" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501154 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{a}$ terms together, but we cannot combine $\\sqrt[3]{a}$ and $\\sqrt{a}$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{a}+9\\sqrt[3]{a}+3\\sqrt[3]{a} & = \\\\ 8\\sqrt{a}+(9+3)\\sqrt[3]{a} & = \\\\ 8\\sqrt{a}+12\\sqrt[3]{a} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501116 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1069 "q-50377" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨4t-10,\\,3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,$ ${r}^{′}(t)=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1072 #uuid "2bf645e6-f706-50ea-8450-5ddbc0499160" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501155 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=4t-10,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3}{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{3}{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501066 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1069 "q-30081" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1071 "![](images/ea/ea7c818a6694ba959c5230dcde8611a7b1dc44bcad383f54c93d2934336c35b6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $9\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $40\\text{ cm}?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1072 #uuid "2d8efcf9-a827-5e33-8982-8c950b1390fe" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501156 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=40\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=40$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(40)^{2}\\cdot (-9)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-43\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$ at the moment when $x=40\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501099 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1069 "q-116519" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1071 "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $144{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $4\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1072 #uuid "38e9b5c8-f7c2-5d27-9b07-5dff34e427b9" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501157 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=144\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 144 & =12(4)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501074 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1069 "q-38861" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1071 "Suppose $T:V\\to W$ is a linear map and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ are the bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W$, respectively. Find the matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the map $T$ relative to these bases, if \n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2},\\,$ $T({b}_{2})={c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,T({b}_{3})=5{c}_{1}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1072 #uuid "3a497a8c-181f-5ddb-8e0c-d7f0dcc66829" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501158 1074 "First, we write the images of vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1})=-3{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{1})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2})={c}_{1}-4{c}_{2} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3})=5{c}_{1} & \\,⟹\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2})$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501018 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1069 "q-75111" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1072 #uuid "3bf6c999-4f9a-599b-886e-437d5517ddbb" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501159 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501061 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1069 "q-119185" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1071 "An oil tank has a leak. The rate of change of the volume $V$ (in liters) of oil in the tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ (in minutes) passed since the tank started leaking. However, the tank is also refilled with $4$ liters of oil every minute. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of oil in the tank at time $t?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1072 #uuid "3d4170a5-9522-5ae2-b33d-b3d78a6d10b4" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501160 1074 "The rate of change in the oil volume decreases in proportion to time $t$ and increases by $4$ liters per minute. The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kt+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501119 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1069 "q-163038" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1071 "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)=5\\sqrt{x},$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1072 #uuid "41337cdf-5450-5e5f-ba7a-c0f13d6a2b84" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501161 1074 "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=5\\sqrt{x},\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 5\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {1}^{5/2}-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501039 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1069 "q-61300" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 25%=90,$ then what is the missing number?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1072 #uuid "449615c3-fdfe-5f50-a3b0-a60b4eb31574" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501162 1074 "The part is $90,$ the percentage is $25%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\n \n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $25%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=\\frac{25÷25}{100÷25}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =90÷25% \\\\  & =90÷\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =90\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =90\\times 4 \\\\  & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501055 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1069 "q-142072" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1071 "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4}$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1072 #uuid "464780e0-2b69-5233-beb8-cdc5f928ae62" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501163 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501026 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1069 "q-71154" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1071 "What is $(-18)^{-1}?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1072 #uuid "4853c7f3-55af-53da-b6a8-b7e1a0edc0bd" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501164 1074 "The expression $(-18)^{-1}$ means the reciprocal of $-18.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(-18)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{18}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501058 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1069 "q-2775" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1071 "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1072 #uuid "4e4521f6-9e6d-5c0e-807a-ed8494a208e7" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501165 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 48 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501031 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1069 "q-140380" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1071 "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1072 #uuid "517afe53-217c-5ede-9811-b9bf164398f6" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501166 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501035 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1069 "q-97637" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1072 #uuid "5b5c204f-7839-5ec6-95c0-a9151439158a" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501167 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,1i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501069 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1069 "q-80579" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-6x+10)$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1072 #uuid "5bc52b75-4c42-58c5-bcab-077cbe6faec2" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501168 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501022 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1069 "q-125649" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1071 "![](images/0f/0f178c33254644c5e2e7bf6f3c596df87b1ea2f0b5bfd417c3f279f6c62b1b0d.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1072 #uuid "5f7aceb3-5a6a-584b-8488-47f8fb82e9a0" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501169 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f87d2e735ad58e0b33a813fb91ab202ac0eeaf90575979ea45f954fb7e1d0493.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/10/102c11f9002e29a3759679c6ff0bd10188908d33eb9cc94a84d900956adc33a5.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501090 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1069 "q-110472" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1071 "Suppose that the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ is applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1072 #uuid "62e49a58-91d5-5a4c-a1e9-c098f0c19c7c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501170 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501059 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1069 "q-109618" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1071 "Write $3-4y=-3x$ in standard form." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1072 #uuid "654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501171 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-4y=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-4y=-3.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501089 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1069 "q-34720" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1071 "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1072 #uuid "66e3887f-e0ed-593b-b271-ae704cd5f2f1" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501172 1074 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2t\\,k \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\sqrt{3}t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501038 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1069 "q-116498" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1071 "![](images/32/324e3b2752fd398e47ce066dfc44f8a4d96cc3d7916f917fa142485ae86bf48f.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the height is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the surface area increasing when the dimensions of the rectangular prism are $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $7\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1072 #uuid "6798c7d5-6dc4-50ce-934a-27d8986f8abb" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501173 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =7\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x,$ $y$ and $z.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2xz+2xy+2yz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2x+2z)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2z+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4)(-5)+(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7)(11)+(2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4)(-5) \\\\  & =-60+198-110 \\\\  & =28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the solid is increasing at a rate of $28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501095 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1069 "q-343321" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1071 "![](images/ba/baa2d9cf6ae9d34de744374dbfe67e44589aa9331c1391e5fc168cd6a39bca4d.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. How fast is the volume of water in the aquarium changing if the water level is rising at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1072 #uuid "68f6bf29-8273-5b7e-a834-752eee067f78" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501174 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the aquarium, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=50\\,\\text{m},\\,W=25\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=0.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(50)(25)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250\\cdot 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501019 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1069 "q-214668" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1071 "What is the slope of the line $4x+7=0?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1072 #uuid "6a919522-1a8b-510f-a55a-40d3694f3593" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501175 1074 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+7 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-7 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line is vertical, and its slope is undefined." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501086 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501123 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1069 "q-214680" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1071 "The line $3x+8y=18$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1072 #uuid "6b2df9ca-452f-5057-9208-dd46b2f5dde7" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501176 1074 "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =18 \\\\ 3x+8(0) & =18 \\\\ 3x & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $x$-intercept is $x=6.$ Thus, the line intersects the $x$-axis at $(6,0).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501092 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1069 "q-2913" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}}$ given that $y>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1072 #uuid "6ef216dc-f6cf-5481-bcc8-f1b9abb1bf84" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501177 1074 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{t}^{8}{y}^{6}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{4}{y}^{3}\\cdot {t}^{4}{y}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{({t}^{4}{y}^{3})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |{y}^{3}| & = \\\\ |{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn even power of a number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have $|{t}^{4}|={t}^{4}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $y>0,$ and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have $|y|=y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{t}^{4}|\\cdot |y{|}^{3}={t}^{4}\\cdot {y}^{3}={t}^{4}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501020 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1069 "q-160355" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 20%=14,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1072 #uuid "706f9bb5-de0a-5c75-aa53-ea495445a284" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501178 1074 "The part is $14,$ the percentage is $20%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $20%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}=\\frac{20÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =14÷20% \\\\  & =14÷\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =14\\times \\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =14\\times 5 \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501028 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1069 "q-143109" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1072 #uuid "70f26979-0b45-521e-afad-0123af31ff5a" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501179 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $y$-axis * is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot |x|,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k|x|.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501056 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1069 "q-134509" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1071 "$t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{5}}+\\sqrt[3]{54{t}^{2}}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1072 #uuid "70fd859a-d33f-544f-b045-d1ae93fdfb95" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501180 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{5}}+\\sqrt[3]{54{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}\\cdot 2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{27\\cdot 2{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}\\cdot 2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}\\cdot 2{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+\\sqrt[3]{{3}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+3\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+t\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}}+3\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ (t+t+3)\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}} & = \\\\ (2t+3)\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{2}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501025 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1069 "q-214661" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1071 "The slope of the line $2y-3=0$ is $m=${{field-1}}." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1072 #uuid "71608d79-53e7-5fb6-8d93-2ff0a25b9a99" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501181 1074 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line is horizontal, and its slope is $m=0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501112 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1069 "q-44202" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,7)$, expressed in standard form?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1072 #uuid "7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501182 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{7-0}{4-0}=\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(0,0).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{7}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y) & =4\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4y & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7x \\\\ -7x+4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+4y & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x-4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $7x-4y=0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501085 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1069 "q-89606" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75}x,\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1072 #uuid "770dc96c-77d3-556b-b303-18d387043365" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501183 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $5\\le x\\le 10,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<5 \\\\ {\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 5\\le x\\le 10 \\\\ 1, & x>10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{5}^{x}\\frac{2}{75}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{75}{t}^{2}|}_{5}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{75}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<5, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{75},\\, & 5\\le x\\le 10, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501070 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1069 "q-22787" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1071 "What is the slope of the line $2x+5y=1?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1072 #uuid "7a0740d0-7284-503e-8e76-e4838bad99ae" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501184 1074 "We can find the slope of the line if we can express its equation in slope-intercept form. To do this, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y & =1 \\\\ 5y & =-2x+1 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{-2x+1}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2x}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the slope-intercept equation, we can tell that the slope of the line is $m=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501129 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1069 "q-116080" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1071 "![](images/e1/e11ec3379c969bbc8499f36978f3a39efe1342ee6563569949dbcb61611ab5f4.png)\n\nA cube of ice is melting uniformly at a rate of $204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ where $x$ is the length of its edge. How fast is each edge of the ice block decreasing when the volume of the cube is $216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1072 #uuid "7b7d80eb-f47e-553b-8d7c-69b51e01b399" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501185 1074 "Let $V$ be the volume of the ice cube. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\n  We start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n  \n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 216 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=6$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -204 & =3(6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{17}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{17}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501076 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1069 "q-131278" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1071 "A supplier of clothing materials looks for flaws before selling the material to customers. On average, there have been $1.7$ flaws per meter. What is the probability of finding fewer than $4$  flaws in the next meter length of the material? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places. You may assume that flaws appear independently and at a constant average rate." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1072 #uuid "7f6be4fe-78ba-5d67-91ec-fd3e20928e4b" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501186 1074 "A Poisson random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\lambda }^{x}{e}^{-\\lambda }}{x!}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of flaws in a meter length of the material. Since the average rate is $1.7$ flaws per meter, and events occur independently and at a constant average rate, the number of flaws can be modeled as a Poisson random variable with rate $\\lambda =1.7,$ and we can write $X∼\\text{Po}(1.7).$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{1.7}^{x}{e}^{-1.7}}{x!}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding fewer than $4$  flaws in the next meter length of the material is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{1.7}^{0}{e}^{-1.7}}{0!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{1}{e}^{-1.7}}{1!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{2}{e}^{-1.7}}{2!}+\\frac{{1.7}^{3}{e}^{-1.7}}{3!} \\\\  & ≈0.907\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501103 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1069 "q-143110" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1072 #uuid "7fc67f3e-fcb8-5740-a171-eb4e59b85b29" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501187 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *square of the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis* is given by ${y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the shortest distance from $P(x,y)$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot {y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501073 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1069 "q-112299" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1071 "Rewrite $-{t}^{2}-12t-7$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1072 #uuid "82dc9974-9e37-5f33-ad85-854a2a504fcc" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501188 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501052 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1069 "q-143130" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1071 "The area of a circle increases at a rate that's inversely proportional to the square of its circumference. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1072 #uuid "842c8f8f-382b-5ffe-9458-677d53445332" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501189 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **increases** at a **rate** that's **inversely proportional** to **the square of its circumference**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate that's *inversely proportional* to *the square of its circumference.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{(2\\pi r)^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4{\\pi }^{2}{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}{r}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{8{\\pi }^{3}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{{r}^{3}}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501023 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501049 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501126 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1069 "q-350227" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1071 "Given that  $f(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)\\,i-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\frac{6}{t}\\,k,$   what is ${f}^{′}(1)?$\n\n${f}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1072 #uuid "87416abb-dad9-5afe-bb9d-e8fcd5a9095c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501190 1074 "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t))\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{6}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{2}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot 1)+2)\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\,j-\\frac{6}{{1}^{2}}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+2\\,i-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501079 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1069 "q-61346" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1071 "On a math test, Kevin answered $95%$ of the problems correctly. If he answered $38$ problems correctly, how many problems were on the test?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1072 #uuid "87c3bda2-8545-5669-83e3-84c3d08222b0" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501191 1074 "We're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of problems answered correctly, which is $38.$ The percentage is $95%,$ and the whole is the total number of problems on the test. \n\nTo solve this question, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $95%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{95}{100}=\\frac{95÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{19}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =38÷95% \\\\  & =38÷\\frac{19}{20} \\\\  & =38\\times \\frac{20}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{19}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $40$ problems on the test." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501075 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1069 "q-140372" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1071 "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+24x$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1072 #uuid "8898e4ea-fd1e-55a2-a1c5-4594ef5b35cf" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501192 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+6x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501124 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1069 "q-143134" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate proportional to the cube of its perimeter. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the side length, $s(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1072 #uuid "8a543fb6-3a0e-5397-9156-31fc05eeae07" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501193 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a square **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **cube of its perimeter**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a square* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the cube of its perimeter*. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=k(4s)^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a square is given by \n\n$$\nA={s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({s}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =k(4s)^{3} \\\\ 2s\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =64k{s}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64k{s}^{3}}{2s} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =32k{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=32k>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}=K{s}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501096 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1069 "q-75033" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨4{t}^{3}-t,\\,7t-3{t}^{4}⟩,$ ${r}^{″}(t)=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1072 #uuid "8b01d0b7-5c1a-5382-b422-a24594a7e8ee" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501194 1074 "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=4{t}^{3}-t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =12{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =24t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=7t-3{t}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =7-12{t}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =-36{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨24t,\\,-36{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501016 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1069 "q-143107" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin. What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1072 #uuid "94332050-ff41-5fa8-9cce-c854bd7e0d1c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501195 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **squared distance from $P$ to the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *squared distance from $P(x,y)$ to the origin* is given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional* to the squared distance from $P$ to the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501088 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1069 "q-314534" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1071 "![](images/81/81175de5197dac2f2f037bde6985be619c68de75a574853c61de75316ae63971.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool is being drained. If its length and width are $32\\text{ m}$ and $12\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.4\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool? Assume that the drain rate is positive and do not round the answer.\n\nThe water is draining out at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1072 #uuid "94a70c07-52ed-54e0-b434-f6f0663ba853" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501196 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=32\\,\\text{m},\\,W=12\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(32)(12)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =384\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-153.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $153.6\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501082 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1069 "q-143142" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. The derivative at the point $(-3,6)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1072 #uuid "9801499d-e8ba-50b1-a83f-440b9081ac28" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501197 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis* is given by $|y|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |y|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|y|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(-3,6).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=k|6|\\,⟹\\,k=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501065 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1069 "q-64096" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1071 "Solve the equation ${2}^{z}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1072 #uuid "9d01c4ee-dae1-5984-a9e0-aab6ea21f976" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501198 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{2}$ as ${2}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{z} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {2}^{z} & ={2}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{z} & ={2}^{-1} \\\\ z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z=-1.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501110 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1069 "q-38598" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1071 "The volume of a sphere increases at a rate proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1072 #uuid "9e12d746-15a9-5bf9-9bc2-9113fc7fbbef" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501199 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a sphere **increases** at a rate **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{4\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501127 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1069 "q-2795" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1071 "![](images/52/52448daf1f9aea06b4ab4713756080ddb3ab465b4fae8f92b65a54c251a81d5e.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $15\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1072 #uuid "a02e865a-6a6d-5b10-8171-4b544dbd46b3" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501200 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ y & =5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ z & =15\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-50{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =(10)(5)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(10)(15)(1)+(15)(5)(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501132 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1069 "q-86542" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1071 "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨2,4⟩?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1072 #uuid "a3fef551-e9bd-56f6-a5fb-6131de39b9cb" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501201 1074 "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{yx})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}y},\\,\\frac{{e}^{y}(y-1)}{x{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =⟨-\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{1}^{2}\\cdot 1},\\,\\frac{{e}^{1}\\cdot (1-1)}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-e,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-e\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501030 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1069 "q-91347" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation ${x}^{3}=-343.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1072 #uuid "a4309379-1166-58a1-b1ee-b77ecf90fd3f" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501202 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =-343 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{-343} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{(-7)^{3}} \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-7.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501108 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1069 "q-134505" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1071 "$3\\sqrt[3]{q}-4\\sqrt{q}+2\\sqrt[3]{q}+2\\sqrt{q}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1072 #uuid "a6334648-97e7-5622-8a2d-3ac7b05ed9c2" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501203 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{q}$ terms together, and the two $\\sqrt{q}$ terms together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt[3]{q}-4\\sqrt{q}+2\\sqrt[3]{q}+2\\sqrt{q} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt[3]{q}+2\\sqrt[3]{q}-4\\sqrt{q}+2\\sqrt{q} & = \\\\ (3+2)\\sqrt[3]{q}+(-4+2)\\sqrt{q} & = \\\\ 5\\sqrt[3]{q}-2\\sqrt{q} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot combine the $\\sqrt[3]{q}$ and $\\sqrt{q}$ terms." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501122 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1069 "q-66523" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1071 "Tom's basketball has a hole in it, meaning its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of a ball. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1072 #uuid "a8090d53-1681-5d0f-a69a-1950471f0a95" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501204 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of the ball **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{(\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{3})}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{3}}{12\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{3}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501111 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1069 "q-214666" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1071 "What is the slope of the line $-4x=5?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1072 #uuid "ab1f13b6-54f8-513e-bf58-990f13684639" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501205 1074 "Note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x & =5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line is vertical, and its slope is undefined." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501029 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1069 "q-140270" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1072 #uuid "aef8db66-e528-55a5-877b-5de5c3121fc3" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501206 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501050 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1069 "q-185091" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}$\\times \\,30%=24.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1072 #uuid "b0060ce0-85fa-5c15-8314-02a2f37da794" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501207 1074 "The part is $24,$ the percentage is $30%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $30%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=\\frac{30÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =24÷30% \\\\  & =24÷\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =24\\times \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{3}\\times 10 \\\\  & =8\\times 10 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $80\\times 30%=24.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501046 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1069 "q-314513" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1071 "![](images/7b/7b45121fc7e3b6de3e37053b944bed610e55d337b7c73d89b258a89f903ad0c2.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $1.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height is increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $9{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ how fast is its width decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{feet}$ in length, $3\\,\\text{feet}$ in width, and $2\\,\\text{feet}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of width is positive.\n\nThe width is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1072 #uuid "b06ab3d9-3b8c-5c9f-a694-75f899defb02" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501208 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501117 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1069 "q-52395" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1071 "$2p\\sqrt{y}-q\\sqrt{y}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1072 #uuid "b1e0dee6-7a44-5abb-b91d-4265ca1d15b0" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501209 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{y}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p\\sqrt{y}-q\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ 2p\\cdot \\sqrt{y}-q\\cdot \\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ (2p-q)\\sqrt{y} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501024 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1069 "q-61343" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1071 "A school's softball team lost $18%$ of the games they played last year. If the team lost $9$ games last year, how many games did the team win?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1072 #uuid "b2281cf7-5deb-5971-96fe-4f78cb0692fd" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501210 1074 "We need to know the total number of games played to determine the number of games won. \n\nWe're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of games the team lost, which is $9.$ The percentage is $18%,$ and the whole is the total number of games played. \n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $18%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{100}=\\frac{18÷2}{100÷2}=\\frac{9}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =9÷18% \\\\  & =9÷\\frac{9}{50} \\\\  & =9\\times \\frac{50}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}\\times 50 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of games played by the softball team last year was $50.$ The total number of games won is\n\n$$\n50-9=41.\n$$\n\nHence, the softball team won $41$ games last year." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501062 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1069 "q-171157" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1071 "$14%$ of{{field-1}}\nis $7.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1072 #uuid "b23ca4fe-ec69-5d5b-b163-9360e0e9626e" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501211 1074 "The part is $7,$ the percentage is $14%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\n \n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $14%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{100}=\\frac{14÷2}{100÷2}=\\frac{7}{50}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =7÷14% \\\\  & =7÷\\frac{7}{50} \\\\  & =7\\times \\frac{50}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7}\\times 50 \\\\  & =1\\times 50 \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14%$ of $50$ is $7.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501097 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1069 "q-89634" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\pi (1+{x}^{6})},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1072 #uuid "b31007d2-c9e1-51d3-8e66-44402735c224" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501212 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{\\pi (1+{t}^{6})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({t}^{3})}{\\pi }|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{3})}{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501034 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1069 "q-72425" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(1,-2)$ and $(3,6)$, expressed in standard form?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1072 #uuid "b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501213 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-(-2)}{3-1}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(1,-2).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4x-4 \\\\ y & =4x-6 \\\\ -4x+y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+y & =-6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ 4x-y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $4x-y=6.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501080 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1069 "q-110493" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1072 #uuid "b525e6cc-0aa9-5691-bec6-c50f5a129f63" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501214 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{2}:=3{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501036 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1069 "q-142029" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{144{b}^{7}}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1072 #uuid "b653142d-595d-5dac-942f-8b41a4d42831" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501215 1074 "We first write $144{b}^{7}$ as the product $144{b}^{6}\\cdot b$ which contains a perfect square $(144{b}^{6}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{144{b}^{7}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{144{b}^{6}\\cdot b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{144{b}^{6}\\cdot b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(12{b}^{3})}^{2}\\cdot b} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(12{b}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(12{b}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{b} & = \\\\ |12{b}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{b} & = \\\\ |12|\\cdot |{b}^{3}|\\cdot \\sqrt{b} & = \\\\ 12|b{|}^{3}\\sqrt{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501121 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1069 "q-141464" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{54{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1072 #uuid "b89f2670-8d2c-52b5-b612-dd1804214943" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501216 1074 "We first write $54{y}^{2}$ as the product $9{y}^{2}\\cdot 6$ which contains a perfect square $(9{y}^{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{54{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{y}^{2}\\cdot 6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9{y}^{2}\\cdot 6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}\\cdot 6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(3y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ |3y|\\cdot \\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{6} & = \\\\ 3|y|\\sqrt{6} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501048 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1069 "q-97578" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSuppose $B$ and $C$ are bases of the vector spaces $V$ and $W,$ respectively. The matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:V\\to W$ relative to these bases, and the coordinates of the vector $x\\in V$ relative to $B$ are shown above. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1072 #uuid "ba29e43e-8fe2-5bbe-a64e-366d83a4fca9" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501217 1074 "The coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501041 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501057 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501063 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1069 "q-218760" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1071 "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1072 #uuid "badd637f-464d-556b-9b5c-ae357a07836a" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501218 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501077 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1069 "q-144820" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin. The derivative at the point $(2,-4)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1072 #uuid "bb3732e8-5921-5cbd-a1fe-55832a8ffa5c" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501219 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *slope of the line through $P(x,y)$ and the origin* is given by $\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the square of the slope of the line through $P$ and the origin, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot {(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(2,-4).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=\\frac{k(-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501128 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1069 "q-4062" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1071 "$3\\sqrt{18b}+3\\sqrt{12b}+2\\sqrt{27b}=$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1072 #uuid "bcc2a2a2-83b1-5938-a74c-5f79d37321a4" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501220 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{18b}+3\\sqrt{12b}+2\\sqrt{27b} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{9\\cdot 2b}+3\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3b}+2\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3b} & = \\\\ 3\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{2b}+3\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3b}+2\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot 3\\cdot \\sqrt{2b}+3\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3b}+2\\cdot 3\\cdot \\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+6\\sqrt{3b}+6\\sqrt{3b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\sqrt{2b}+6\\sqrt{3b}+6\\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+(6+6)\\sqrt{3b} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{2b}+12\\sqrt{3b} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501134 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1069 "q-72" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1071 "Solve the equation ${5}^{x}=\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1072 #uuid "c1a2b990-ffe2-5008-9b55-fe50c6e1b0a9" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501221 1074 "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{5}$ as ${5}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {5}^{x} & ={5}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x} & ={5}^{-1} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501051 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1069 "q-141477" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{44{u}^{8}}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1072 #uuid "c2d5742a-0261-5618-8222-f4f0f769fb79" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501222 1074 "We first write $44{u}^{8}$ as the product $4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11$ which contains a perfect square $(4{u}^{8}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{44{u}^{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we separate the product into two separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{u}^{8}\\cdot 11} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}\\cdot 11} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the radical containing the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(2{u}^{4})}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ |2{u}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{u}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{11} & = \\\\ 2|u{|}^{4}\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501043 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501064 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1069 "q-349466" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1071 "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,{t}^{2}+4t⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(t)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1072 #uuid "c6495e61-0973-5b35-994b-13978ddf3f2b" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501223 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)={t}^{2}+4t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨{x}^{′}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,2t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501093 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1069 "q-111446" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation ${x}^{3}=-512.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1072 #uuid "cb6a3551-b7c8-5448-9dd7-361a94a204ac" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501224 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =-512 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{-512} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{(-8)^{3}} \\\\ x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-8.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501104 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1069 "q-185531" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x|}}{2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1072 #uuid "d0e0e171-2942-5666-9f4c-984a9e7d868a" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501225 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501114 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1069 "q-110919" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{4{f}^{2}{g}^{2}{h}^{2}}$ given that $f<0,$ $g<0$ and $h>0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1072 #uuid "d271c02f-63d9-52da-abf5-d399868f3744" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501226 1074 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{f}^{2}{g}^{2}{h}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2fgh)}^{2}} & = \\\\ |2fgh| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |fgh| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |fgh| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $f<0,$ $g<0$ and $h>0.$ We know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |fgh| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |f|\\cdot |g|\\cdot |h| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot (-f)\\cdot (-g)\\cdot (h) & = \\\\ 2fgh.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501084 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1069 "q-83562" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1072 #uuid "d2926681-2f02-501e-9c4c-db877ffeff07" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501227 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501027 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1069 "q-49012" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1071 "![](images/06/06eb8269e36ecb0185c0bfa0069c36a705e36711ca0a705fba3e6eae7b6c946d.png)\n\nJohn is a carpenter who wants to modify a wooden box. The length, width and height of the box are decreased at a rate of $1\\text{ cm/s},$ $2\\text{ cm/s}$ and $4\\text{ cm/s},$ respectively. Determine the rate at which the volume of the box decreases, if its dimensions are $15\\text{ cm}$ long, $10\\text{ cm}$ wide and $15\\text{ cm}$ high." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1072 #uuid "dd9599f1-ed67-5948-967c-4c445ad154d3" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501228 1074 "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=15\\text{ cm},\\, & W=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=15\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(10)(15)(-1)+(15)(15)(-2)+(15)(10)(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the box is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501053 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1069 "q-111453" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $(x-5)^{3}=-27.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1072 #uuid "e173cb82-cae9-5da1-9ec4-a1b561689a6a" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501229 1074 "We can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$\nSince $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{3} & =-27 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x-5)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{-27} \\\\ x-5 & =\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}} \\\\ x-5 & =-3 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=2.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501078 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1069 "q-25254" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1071 "Rewrite $-19-14z-{z}^{2}$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1072 #uuid "e1ed5444-a38b-5b2e-8f5a-4a0e97b37ff2" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501230 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({z}^{2}+14z+19)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+14z+19\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+19\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${7}^{2},$ writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+{7}^{2}}_{⏟}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-30\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19-14z-{z}^{2} & =-((z+7)^{2}-30) \\\\  & =-(z+7)^{2}+30 \\\\  & =30-(z+7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501068 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1069 "q-52504" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}}\\,$ given that $y<0.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1072 #uuid "e44f5e7f-50c8-5f61-b0b8-699aaec6f842" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501231 1074 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{y}\\cdot \\frac{3}{y}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{y})}^{2}} & = \\\\ |\\frac{3}{y}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|}{|y|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y<0.$ To take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have $|y|=-y.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{|y|}=-\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501102 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1069 "q-343194" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1071 "![](images/99/996768e4b1ce8ae57bcf3cce61059f8349c307ea9f05c9c1f51831ff6fd8ae68.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed. The length of the loaf is decreasing by $20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},$ while the $60\\,\\text{mm}$ width does not change. If the volume of the loaf is constant, how fast is its height changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1072 #uuid "e4e15d3e-925b-56d4-bf57-253dd32acaf7" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501232 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =100\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ y & =60\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ z & =30\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =0\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is not changing and $y=60,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=60xz.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60xz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =60x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60z(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =60(100)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60(30)(-20) \\\\ 0 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})-36\\,000 \\\\ 36\\,000 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the solid is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501060 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1069 "q-111455" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1071 "Find the real solution to the equation $3(x+7)^{9}=1\\,536.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1072 #uuid "ea193efd-d8e8-59fc-adc1-1d1595da20e0" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501233 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+7)^{9}$ term and then taking the $9$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+7)^{9}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+7)^{9} & =1\\,536 \\\\ (x+7)^{9} & =512\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $9$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=9$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)^{9} & =512 \\\\ \\sqrt[9]{(x+7)^{9}} & =\\sqrt[9]{512} \\\\ x+7 & =\\sqrt[9]{{2}^{9}} \\\\ x+7 & =2 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-5.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501045 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1069 "q-97670" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1072 #uuid "ea3775f0-4a19-53d1-b4e1-4eb3518e099f" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501234 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{27}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={3}^{2}\\cdot 3=27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501105 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1069 "q-160895" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1071 "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,-2),$ $(3,-1),$ and $(1,1).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1072 #uuid "ea3ea5b4-3a71-55fd-af96-eb417a578734" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501235 1074 "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=(x-y){e}^{x+y}\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(0,0) & =(0-0,0+0) \\\\  & =(0,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-2) & =(2-(-2),2+(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-1) & =(3-(-1),3+(-1)) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,1) & =(1-1,1+1) \\\\  & =(0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/0b/0b23c6463447aa1fb64bdeeca689a40711f8dad59b348bb7943c2b9d2de7e8fc.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y){e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u{e}^{v}\\,|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}u{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{0}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =4({e}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501113 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1069 "q-140420" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1071 "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-7x-11$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1072 #uuid "eaa23e3b-bd80-5fbc-b343-c6e4d7d3776d" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501236 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501015 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1069 "q-128991" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1071 "Calculate the $y$-intercept of the line $4x-6y=-15.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1072 #uuid "eaf53385-8d80-57b1-9850-e91d1aa3e647" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501237 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation and solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6y & =-15 \\\\ 4(0)-6y & =-15 \\\\ -6y & =-15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501040 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501067 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501087 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1069 "q-347967" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1071 "Determine ${f}^{″}(t)$ given that $f(t)={e}^{5-2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,j-3{t}^{4}\\,k.$\n\n${f}^{″}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$i\\,\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$j\\,\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$k$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1072 #uuid "eb16de2e-8644-5cec-a8df-6022a66874f3" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501238 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{5-2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{4})\\,k \\\\  & =-2{e}^{5-2t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,j-12{t}^{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThen, we calculate ${f}^{″}(t)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{5-2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-12{t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =-2(-2{e}^{5-2t})\\,i+3(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,j-12(3{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =4{e}^{5-2t}\\,i-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,j-36{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501072 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1069 "q-75161" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1071 "Suppose that the following elementary row operations are applied to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ in the given order:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1072 #uuid "f10c9aaf-98e6-52a7-9de1-c8d29489c892" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501239 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501021 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501133 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1069 "q-214677" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1071 "The line $3x-14y=-3$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1072 #uuid "f153c106-d98f-5f2d-af98-3aa7927bfc4d" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501240 1074 "To find the $x$-intercept, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation and solve for $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-14y & =-3 \\\\ 3x-14(0) & =-3 \\\\ 3x & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the $x$-intercept is $x=-1.$ Thus, the line intersects the $x$-axis at $(-1,0).$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501044 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1069 "q-343180" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1071 "![](images/49/4946691a4258fb310ec7052f1edd5a1b9117ca7daeea152e4a1b01b11a624e2e.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe water level is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1072 #uuid "f3d27d1e-a375-5722-8f78-27b79ebf66c3" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501241 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =50\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =25\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=50\\,\\text{cm}$ and $y=25\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(50)(25)z=1250z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1250z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}250 & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =0.2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is increasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501047 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1069 "q-34722" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1071 "Calculate the second derivative of  $f(s)=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s⟩$ with respect to $s.$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1072 #uuid "f415e47a-854d-5840-b197-b8e66cc67e42" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501242 1074 "First, we find the derivative of $f(s)$ by differentiating each component.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate ${f}^{″}(s)$ by differentiating ${f}^{′}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4s)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4s⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501120 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1069 "q-140379" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1071 "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1072 #uuid "fc2b2629-e323-5ab5-9be3-1b12132dec56" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501243 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501244 1058 #edb/ref 17592186501248 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501244 1062 #uuid "0408ea5d-a169-5ec2-b15a-fbb75152c5cb" 13194139535348 17592186045417 true] [17592186501244 1063 #edb/ref 17592186501158 13194139535348 175921